WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.200
Training and inference efficiency should be measured as useful output per dollar, not simply as maximum hardware activity.

2
00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:16.800
A busy GPU is not automatically cost-effective if the work is low value, the run is misconfigured, or the result is not usable.

3
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:25.200
For training, batch size, model size, data loading, checkpoint frequency, precision choices, runtime settings, and distributed coordination can all affect utilization.

4
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.000
The goal is not to tune endlessly.

5
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:35.600
The goal is to find the point where the workload produces reliable results with reasonable cost and operational complexity.

6
00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:38.000
For inference, demand patterns matter.

7
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.800
Caching can reduce repeated work when responses or intermediate results are reusable.

8
00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:45.600
Batching can improve throughput for some services.

9
00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:55.600
Autoscaling can help when it follows real demand, but it can waste money if it reacts to noisy signals or keeps too much capacity warm.

10
00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:02.400
Smaller models, optimized deployments, or different serving patterns may meet the same business need at lower cost.

11
00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:08.800
Those choices should be evaluated against quality, latency, safety, and reliability requirements, not only raw spend.

12
00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:13.200
Efficiency work is most useful when it is connected to measurement.

13
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:22.000
Teams should know the cost per run, cost per batch, cost per evaluation, or cost per useful request where that is practical.

14
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.800
That turns optimization from guesswork into engineering.

15
00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:28.000
The safest efficiency program makes small, measurable changes.

16
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:34.000
Adjust one setting, compare results, and keep the version that meets requirements with less waste.

17
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.800
That approach avoids trading away reliability for a short-term cost drop.
